{"combines": true, "proposition": "Across the Se-doped series, low lattice-plus-bipolar thermal conductivity (kappa_L+kappa_b ~0.69 W/mK at 300 K for x=0.01, falling to ~0.60 at 400-450 K, then rising above ~475 K for all doped samples) co-occurs with elevated room-temperature ZT in the doped series (0.84 for x=0.005, 0.78 for x=0.01, 0.63-0.69 for x=0.02-0.04), approaching but not surpassing the n-Bi2Te3 reference (~0.88). Together the two panels support a general association: suppressed thermal transport accompanies enhanced figure of merit across the doped composition series.", "limits": ["kappa_L+kappa_b is reported only for x=0.01, while the highest ZT (~0.84) occurs at x=0.005; the pairing cannot establish that low kappa_L specifically at x=0.005 produced that peak ZT because no kappa value is given for that composition -- a composition mismatch between the two panels", "the temperature windows differ: kappa_L+kappa_b spans 300-450 K (with rise above ~475 K), while the ZT values compared are all at 300 K only, so the combination cannot show how any kappa_L-ZT relationship behaves outside room temperature", "ZT is a composite figure of merit that depends on additional transport parameters not given in these two observations, so the co-occurring trend does not isolate lattice thermal conductivity as the sole or dominant contributor to the ZT differences across x"], "not_identifiable": null, "quantity": {"a": {"kind": "material transport property (thermal conductivity), W/m·K, temperature series 300-450 K plus rise above 475 K", "what": "kappa_L+kappa_b for x=0.01 doped sample"}, "b": {"kind": "dimensionless composite/normalized figure of merit, single temperature point (300 K), across compositions", "what": "ZT at 300 K for x=0.005, 0.01, 0.02-0.04, and n-Bi2Te3 reference"}}, "labels": {"dependency": "real", "inference_validity": "follows, weakly", "causal_strength": "associative"}}